;; Docs for std/monad.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "monad.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The Monad trait, instances for List, Option, and Result, and do-notation.")
  (see pure bind do)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std monad))

    (: calc (-> int (Option int)))
    (defun (calc n)
      (do (:bind half (safe-div n 2))
          (:let  doubled (* half 2))
          (:bind third (safe-div doubled 3))
          (:return (+ doubled third))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A monad is a type constructor that supports two core operations:
    @code{pure} lifts a regular value into the monad, and @code{bind} sequences
    computations by passing an inner value to a function returning the next monad.
    The @code{(do ...)} macro provides convenient sequential step syntax over
    chained @code{bind} calls.

    @strong{Standard instances.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{List}: @code{bind} applies the function to each element and
        concatenates the resulting lists in order.}
      @item{@code{Option}: @code{bind} short-circuits on @code{None}. The block
        returns @code{None} if any step evaluates to @code{None}.}
      @item{@code{Result}: @code{bind} short-circuits on @code{Err}. The block
        returns the first @code{Err} without executing subsequent steps.}
    }

    The return type of the block determines which instance is used.
    """))

(:doc Monad
  (summary "Trait for sequencing monadic computations via pure and bind.")
  (see pure bind do)
  (reference """
    Requires implementing @code{pure} and @code{bind} for a type constructor
    (such as @code{List} or @code{Option}), enabling @code{(do ...)} syntax for
    that type.
    """))

(:doc pure
  (summary "Lifts a value into a monadic context.")
  (arg x "The value to wrap.")
  (returns "A monad wrapping x: (Cons x Nil), (Some x), or (Ok x).")
  (see bind do))

(:doc bind
  (summary "Sequences monadic computations by applying a function to the contained value.")
  (arg m "The monadic value.")
  (arg k "Continuation function taking an inner value and returning a monad. May be a fun or bjoroutine.")
  (returns "The resulting monad from k. For List, concatenates all results in order. For None or Err, returns m unchanged without calling k.")
  (see pure do)
  (example """
    (bind (Some 2) (fun (x) (Some (* x 10))))   ; (Some 20)
    (bind (list 1 2) (fun (x) (list x x)))      ; (1 1 2 2)
    """))

(:doc do
  (summary "Sequences monadic operations using step-based do-notation.")
  (form (do step ...))
  (rest step """
    A step, one of:

    @code{(:bind name expr)}: binds @code{name} to the unwrapped value of
    monad @code{expr}. @code{name} must be an identifier.

    @code{(:match pattern expr)}: like @code{:bind}, but destructures the
    unwrapped value against @code{pattern}. Mismatches raise an exception.

    @code{(:let name expr)}: binds @code{name} directly to @code{expr} without
    monadic unwrapping.

    @code{(:then expr)}: evaluates the monad @code{expr} for side effects and
    discards its inner value.

    @code{(:return expr)}: valid in final step position only. Returns
    @code{(pure expr)}.

    Any other expression: evaluated for side effects without unwrapping. In the
    final position, it determines the block's return value and must evaluate to
    a monad.
    """)
  (see bind pure)
  (example """
    ;; All sums of pairs from two lists.
    (do (:bind x (list 1 2))
        (:bind y (list 10 20))
        (:return (+ x y)))          ; (11 21 12 22)

    ;; Stops at the first Err.
    (do (:match (Tuple w h) (parse-pair text))
        (:bind width (checked w))
        (:bind height (checked h))
        (:return (* width height)))
    """)
  (reference """
    The block requires at least one step. The final step defines the block's
    result: either an explicit @code{:return} or an expression returning a
    monad. Binding steps (@code{:bind}, @code{:match}, @code{:let}, or
    @code{:then}) cannot appear in the final position. Unrecognized keywords in
    step position produce syntax errors.

    The final step can be a recursive call returning a monad, allowing tail
    calls in monadic loops.

    Steps following @code{:bind}, @code{:match}, or @code{:then} compile into
    the body of a lambda passed to @code{bind}, and therefore cannot suspend.
    """))
